WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.110
- Ah.
(audience cheers)

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:01.980
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.110
So, okay, I'm very
excited for you personally

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.390
because I remember the
first time we Zoomed.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.260
You were actually at your house

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.307
in Hawaii, I think.
- That's right, yeah.

00:00:08.307 --> 00:00:10.830
- And I was like, "Why do
I not live where you live?"

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.630
And then also there was
art in the background.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.396
We had this whole conversation
about how you love painting,

00:00:14.396 --> 00:00:16.470
and I had no idea about
this background of yours.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.720
And now this is your
first like ever, right,

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.240
like solo like art exhibition?
- This is it.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.860
This is it.
- You're so crazy good, man.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.652
- May 13th, thank you, Kelly.

00:00:24.652 --> 00:00:26.878
Yes, one tries to be, at least

00:00:26.878 --> 00:00:28.837
to be an unexpected surprise.
- It's incredible.

00:00:28.837 --> 00:00:30.972
"So Many Dreams," I know
that's the name of it, right?

00:00:30.972 --> 00:00:33.143
- That's right, yeah.
- That is one of my favorites.

00:00:33.143 --> 00:00:35.730
I love this storytelling behind it too

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.660
'cause I watched this whole video

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.940
about you describing like your roots

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.980
and like, you know, why you paint

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.000
and the stories behind them.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.170
It's really beautiful.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:44.430
- Yeah, it's just, you know,

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.800
I've been an enthusiastic
painter all my life,

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.060
as a friend of mine says,

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.850
and this is my first exhibition.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.590
I've had this body of work.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.000
It's a retrospective.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.243
- Yeah.
- Starting in 1987, really.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.110
And I dropped into LA in 1982.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.420
I came here, I got a job, I
got lucky, "Remington Steele."

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.690
- Yeah.
- And I stayed.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.970
And, you know, I could afford paints

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.179
and brushes even though I'd never painted,

00:01:12.179 --> 00:01:14.700
but I loved drawing.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.290
I bought all these art supplies,

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.180
and they all went into a cupboard.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.410
- I heard you say this in your video,

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.180
and I was like that's so
everyone though, right?

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.220
It's like you work hard to make the money

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.140
to be able to afford the things
you love and you wanna do,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.250
but then you don't have the time

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.510
because you're working so hard

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:28.343
to do it.
- Exactly.

00:01:28.343 --> 00:01:30.630
- Yeah, that stinks 'cause
it goes in the closet.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:31.650
How long was it in the closet

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.839
until you pulled it back out?

00:01:33.839 --> 00:01:36.600
- (chuckles) It was there until '87.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.340
And my wife Cassie, God bless her,

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.404
she got sick with ovarian cancer,

00:01:40.404 --> 00:01:45.404
and then one dark night
with just this kind of head

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.570
and a heart full of pain and fear,

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.450
I just got all these paints
out and I started painting,

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.870
and those paintings start the show,

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.390
and there's some kind of
articulation, I think,

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.640
in some progression of the work.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:00.958
And they've all been in storage,

00:02:00.958 --> 00:02:03.450
and I thought I have to let them go.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.980
I mean, if not now, when?

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.720
Otherwise, they're just gonna sit there.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.760
- It's such a cool way
to share with everybody,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:09.840
to connect with everybody

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.570
because I forgot that I used to draw.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:13.440
I just forgot.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.530
And then I literally found
myself doing something

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:17.700
that I had to kind of pay attention to

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:18.660
and I was having a hard time,

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.700
and then all of a sudden
I've doodled this whole thing

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.470
by the end of this thing.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:23.940
And I was like, oh, I forgot I even drew,

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.185
but I was doing it as like
almost a therapeutic way.

00:02:26.185 --> 00:02:27.770
Do you do it a lot for that?

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:29.346
- It is definitely therapy for me.

00:02:29.346 --> 00:02:30.503
It kind of-

00:02:30.503 --> 00:02:33.120
- Because I heard your wife gardens.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.882
- My darling Keely, she gardens, yes.

00:02:35.882 --> 00:02:36.715
- And you paint

00:02:36.715 --> 00:02:38.160
I love this therapy, yeah.
- I paint, yeah, so...

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.740
And we have this little
homestead out there in Kauai.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.380
So that's the heartbeat
of our life really.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.480
I think we live in a time,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.180
which has been very...

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.380
The thump of anxiety in our
lives in these days is constant,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.377
and I follow a lot of
artists on Instagram.

00:02:55.377 --> 00:02:58.260
And so many people now,
whether they're weaving

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.290
or they're linocutting or painting,

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.090
you know, it's necessary

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.157
to have art in your life I think.

00:03:06.157 --> 00:03:07.260
- 100%.
- Creative life.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.242
I would not have made
it the last three years

00:03:09.242 --> 00:03:11.400
without being able to write music.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.750
Like that's my outlet, right.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.720
And I actually heard that
you wanted to do album covers

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.490
was like a big thing for
you whenever you started.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.310
- Well, yes, I mean, I left
school at 16 with nothing

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.880
but a cardboard folder of
drawings and paintings.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.670
That was my qualification.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:30.480
I got a job in Putney, South
London, and it was '69,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.882
so the music, the world of Carlos Santana

00:03:33.882 --> 00:03:37.200
was great albums covers,
and that was my dream

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.637
to make album covers.

00:03:38.637 --> 00:03:43.260
It didn't happen, but my
son, who's a musician,

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.767
he asked me to do his album cover, so...

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:47.040
- [Kelly] I know I was
gonna bring that up.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.100
How full circle and also
such a beautiful thing

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.477
for you to do with your kid.

00:03:51.477 --> 00:03:53.318
(audience applauds)
- Yeah, thank you.

00:03:53.318 --> 00:03:54.685
- Like I have kids, and I can only imagine

00:03:54.685 --> 00:03:57.480
like my kid would ask me
to be a part of something,

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.120
you know, 'cause sometimes
we're not cool. (laughs)

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:01.442
- Sometimes we're not cool, yeah,

00:04:01.442 --> 00:04:03.090
and it takes a long time to be cool

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.070
and then uncool at the same time.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.290
But anyway, yeah, that's-

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.090
- What a great time for
that, that happened.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:10.023
- It is.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:12.480
A lot of these paintings come

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.680
from a certain kind of
therapeutic anxiety,

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:20.130
nourishment of the soul, and I love it.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:22.813
- That's important, yeah.
- That's where you begin with

00:04:22.813 --> 00:04:25.920
you love the song, the
poem that you've written.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.953
It starts in your heart,
the painting that you make,

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.260
and then you give it away.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:32.093
- Absolutely.

00:04:32.093 --> 00:04:33.100
(audience cheer)
So Pierce is not only

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.610
a great actor, but he's an amazing artist.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:39.360
His first ever solo art exhibition
called "So Many Dreams,"

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.830
which is the coolest title ever,

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:45.810
starts Sunday, May 13th at 434
North La Brea in Los Angeles.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.600
While we've all lived many
lives, especially actors,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.143
probably not as many as you, man,

00:04:50.143 --> 00:04:52.350
and because we are always evolving,

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.600
we're never quite the
same person we used to be.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.570
It is time to find out who
Pierce Brosnan used to be, y'all.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:00.330
As we look back through
past snapshots of his life,

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.040
I'm very excited about this title.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.423
This is Pierced by Pierce.

00:05:04.427 --> 00:05:07.344
♪ Pierce by Pierce ♪

00:05:08.733 --> 00:05:11.270
- Okay, Jessi.
(audience applauds)

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:13.350
Anyways, so I'm gonna hit this button

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.170
and a certain Pierce from the past,

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:17.850
say that five times fast, will appear

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.610
on that screen, and
Pierce now will tell us

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.770
about Pierce then.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.180
It's very "Inception."

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:25.455
Okay, here's the first Pierce.

00:05:25.455 --> 00:05:28.444
(screen toggles)

00:05:28.444 --> 00:05:30.090
(bell dings)
All right, here we go.

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:31.350
- Oh my God.
- First of all,

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.150
I wanna ride that. (laughs)

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.010
- Okay, so I'm finished at drama school,

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:36.330
three years of drama school.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.930
I'm trained in the
method, a serious actor.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.820
However, you have to make a living.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.860
I found a commercials agent,

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:47.130
and you had to have
photographs doing silly things.

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:49.050
And that's one of them.
(audience laughs)

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:49.883
- [Kelly] That's not silly.

00:05:49.883 --> 00:05:50.880
That looks fun.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.482
- So, so goofy.

00:05:52.482 --> 00:05:56.400
Yes, that was me trying
to get commercial work.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.020
- Can I tell you my personal favorite part

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.810
is the fancy sweater look
that you just finished

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:03.210
at tennis club, and then your scooter.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.710
You know, you don't
have a Porsche outside.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.247
You have this scooter outside.

00:06:06.247 --> 00:06:08.250
(both laugh)
It's great.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:09.480
That builds character.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:10.313
Here we go.

00:06:10.313 --> 00:06:12.702
(screen toggles)

00:06:12.702 --> 00:06:14.130
Okay, what's the next one?
(bell dings)

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:14.963
Oh, "Remington Steele."

00:06:14.963 --> 00:06:19.080
- Oh, my dear, dear Stephanie.
(audience cheers)

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:20.670
Stephanie Zimbalist and I.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.410
This was a turning point in my life.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.060
It created a whole world for me.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.040
It gave me the life and career

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:26.970
that I have-
- Was this your first series

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.890
here in America?
- This was my first series.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:32.550
I came over here, wow, as I say, in 1982

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.530
on a wing and a prayer, my late wife,

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:37.620
and she, God bless her, said,
"We should go to America."

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.517
I said, "How the heck are
we gonna go to America?

00:06:39.517 --> 00:06:41.520
"We just bought a house."

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.040
And we borrowed two
grand, hopped on a plane,

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.214
came over here to Los Angeles.

00:06:45.214 --> 00:06:46.526
- Both dreamers

00:06:46.526 --> 00:06:49.620
- And stayed in Hollywood.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:52.286
I rented a car from Rent-A-Wreck.

00:06:52.286 --> 00:06:53.300
- [Kelly] (laughs) I love it.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:54.133
- It was a lime green Pacer.

00:06:54.133 --> 00:06:55.680
- You should have brought
the scooter. (laughs)

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.700
- Should have brought it. (whistles)

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:02.040
- I'm just picturing James
Bond on the scooter. (laughs)

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.210
It's incredible.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.560
My son's name is Remington.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:05.393
I didn't even put it together

00:07:05.393 --> 00:07:07.160
until obviously that's
like what you came to.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.090
I was like, that's my son's name.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:10.410
- Well, I went for the first interview,

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:13.350
and that was "Remington
Steele," and I did the job,

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:14.383
so there you have it.

00:07:14.383 --> 00:07:16.179
- I think it was a hit.
(audience cheers)

00:07:16.179 --> 00:07:17.012
- Thank you.

00:07:17.012 --> 00:07:17.865
All right, let's see the next one.

00:07:17.865 --> 00:07:20.574
(screen toggles)

00:07:20.574 --> 00:07:21.967
(bell dings)
(Kelly murmurs)

00:07:21.967 --> 00:07:23.760
Oh, there you go.
(audience cheers)

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:25.320
Everyone loved "Mrs. Doubtfire."

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:26.670
I don't know what a human on this planet

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:27.810
that doesn't love "Mrs. Doubtfire."

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:28.680
So it must have been something else

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.680
to work with Robin, right?
- It was such a blast,

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:32.583
so memorable.

00:07:32.583 --> 00:07:35.040
I mean, if I never did anything else,

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.010
I made "Mrs. Doubtfire," and this job came

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:39.780
at a time where I was really worrying

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.640
about the mortgage payment, (laughs),

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:44.430
and they said, "You got a
job with Robin Williams."

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:49.430
And it was truly magnificent experience.

00:07:50.068 --> 00:07:52.350
I remember the first
day up in San Francisco,

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:53.977
it was a gorgeous sunny
day, and they said,

00:07:53.977 --> 00:07:56.580
"Well, welcome, and do
you wanna meet Robin?"

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:57.450
I said, "Yeah."

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:59.070
And he said, "He's in the makeup trailer."

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.165
So I went into the makeup trailer,

00:08:01.165 --> 00:08:02.640
and he was sitting there

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.700
and he had a Hawaiian
shirt on and big hairy arms

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:09.840
and legs and cargo pants,
but Mrs. Doubtfire, his head.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:11.130
- That's so creepy.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:11.963
It's really weird.

00:08:11.963 --> 00:08:13.057
"Oh, hello, Pierce.

00:08:13.057 --> 00:08:14.073
"Oh, give us a kiss."

00:08:14.073 --> 00:08:16.117
(Kelly laughs)

00:08:16.117 --> 00:08:17.557
"Hey, buddy, nice to see you.

00:08:17.557 --> 00:08:18.390
"How you doing?"

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:19.380
"Hey, oh, high five."

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:20.580
- Revert back. (laughs)

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.370
- So, and I really
never worked with Robin.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.230
I worked with Mrs.
Doubtfire the whole time

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:27.600
because he was in makeup at
four o'clock in the morning.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.160
I'd come in for my work, and-

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:29.993
- Oh, that's so funny.

00:08:29.993 --> 00:08:32.310
- And so, I never saw Robin.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:34.260
It was always Mrs. Doubtfire.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:36.294
- It just must be cool as
an actor to be a part of.

00:08:36.294 --> 00:08:38.598
I mean, God, you've been a
part of so many huge things,

00:08:38.598 --> 00:08:41.310
but just something that
I feel like families,

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:43.650
that's just that film
was such a huge thing

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:44.529
in pop culture for families.

00:08:44.529 --> 00:08:46.590
Like, it doesn't matter what age you were,

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:47.423
everybody loved that movie.

00:08:47.423 --> 00:08:49.380
- No, it's a very healing film.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:50.610
Very joyful film.

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:52.080
And it has lasted

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:53.423
the test of time.
- Yeah, classic, yeah.

00:08:53.423 --> 00:08:56.760
- It is a classic, so to
be part of that, brilliant.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:57.593
- Well, I'm moving on.

00:08:57.593 --> 00:08:58.711
Here we go, next Pierce.

00:08:58.711 --> 00:09:00.172
(screen toggles)

00:09:00.172 --> 00:09:01.005
- Oh, this is like

00:09:01.005 --> 00:09:01.838
"This Is Your Life."
(bell dings)

00:09:01.838 --> 00:09:03.290
- I know, I remember that show, yeah.

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:04.194
(audience cheers)

00:09:04.194 --> 00:09:06.540
"This Is Your Life" (indistinct).

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:08.370
Oh my God, I have to ask you about this.

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:10.080
I love "Mamma Mia!"

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.611
I have never seen a more stacked cast.

00:09:13.611 --> 00:09:16.320
- It's truly stacked cast.
- That cast was

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.040
every person that came on
camera was like a legend.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:21.930
Like it was an incredible
cast to be part of.

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:23.550
Were you nervous about singing or excited?

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:24.383
- Oh, I was nervous.

00:09:24.383 --> 00:09:25.216
I was terrified.

00:09:25.216 --> 00:09:26.049
I mean, I did-
- Really?

00:09:26.049 --> 00:09:27.090
- Oh, I was-
- You did such a great job.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:28.650
- Terrified, thank you for saying so.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:30.270
I'd never sung in a movie before,

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:34.890
but this movie really was a game changer.

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:38.040
And it's easy to fall in love with Meryl.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:38.873
It's easy to fall in love

00:09:38.873 --> 00:09:40.650
with every member of the cast.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:42.150
Yes, truly.
- And Colin.

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:45.180
I mean, everybody in that
movie is so great, yeah.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:46.516
- It was criminal how much fun we had

00:09:46.516 --> 00:09:48.420
because everyone brought their wives,

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:49.393
their girlfriends, the boyfriends.

00:09:49.393 --> 00:09:51.960
- Oh, that's cool.
- So it became a family.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:54.630
And we rehearsed for about five weeks.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:57.245
Everyone got on extremely well together.

00:09:57.245 --> 00:09:59.225
So maybe "Mamma Mia 3."

00:09:59.225 --> 00:10:01.040
We're talking about it.
- Let's do it.

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:02.141
(audience cheers)
Let's do it.

00:10:02.141 --> 00:10:04.399
Okay, last Pierce, here we go.

00:10:04.399 --> 00:10:06.295
(screen toggles)

00:10:06.295 --> 00:10:07.860
(bell dings)

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.150
Okay, wait, this is the new one.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:09.983
- This is the new one.

00:10:09.983 --> 00:10:12.328
This is "The Out-Laws"
with Adam DeVine there.

00:10:12.328 --> 00:10:15.185
Ellen Barkin, and Nina Dobrev.

00:10:15.185 --> 00:10:18.180
And this is coming out July 7th,

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.730
and it's a romp.
(audience cheers)

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:23.250
- So I love that it's
called "The Out-Laws."

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.350
So is it because you're in-laws,

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:26.803
but you might be, is it bank robbers?

00:10:26.803 --> 00:10:29.940
- Well, it's a kind of
spinoff of "The In-Laws,"

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:32.310
which is an old movie,
but we're the out-laws.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:34.980
And Adam DeVine, he's
a nerdy bank manager.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:37.170
He's getting married to his lovely girl.

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:39.750
Her parents are coming in, Ellen and I,

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:41.700
and we've been in...

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:46.080
We supposedly have been in
the Amazon studying a tribe,

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:47.940
but really we're bank robbers.
- Possibly.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:48.773
- We're bank robbers.

00:10:48.773 --> 00:10:52.620
We come in to rob banks.
- That's incredible.

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:54.060
- It's a romp of a movie.

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:55.130
So much fun.
- Seems very witty.

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:58.770
- It's witty, it's fast, it's comedic.

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:01.810
It's a great ensemble, again, of actors

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:05.190
and such a wonderful time doing it, so...

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:06.540
- I like you in comedies,

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:07.980
and the "Eurovision" one too,

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:09.150
that was one of the last times we talked.

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:11.100
I loved "Eurovision."
- Oh, that was so silly.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:12.536
- I mean, you actually
made me cry in that too.

00:11:12.536 --> 00:11:14.700
You were funny, and you made me cry.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:15.540
So well done.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:18.000
Theaters worked out for you.
- That's good.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.040
- But, no, I love you in comedies,

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:21.270
so I love that you're doing that.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:23.340
All right, that was our game.

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:24.328
You're welcome, America.

00:11:24.328 --> 00:11:25.177
(audience cheers)
- That's all, thank you.

00:11:25.177 --> 00:11:29.040
- "The Out-Laws: is on Netflix in July,

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:31.470
and don't forget to check
out Pierce's art exhibition.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.210
It's called "So Many Dreams."

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:34.043
He's incredible.

00:11:34.043 --> 00:11:35.347
It starts Sunday, May 13th

00:11:35.347 --> 00:11:38.583
at 434 North La Brea in Los Angeles.

